WEBVTT

00:00:01.500 --> 00:00:03.580
My mother believed in using things up.
我母亲信奉东西要物尽其用。

00:00:04.380 --> 00:00:09.200
Last year, our cornfield had a bad harvest because birds feasted on the crops.
去年，鸟儿把庄稼吃了个痛快，我们家的玉米地收成很差。

00:00:10.000 --> 00:00:14.240
It was probably then that Mom’s frugality became even more extraordinary.
大概就是从那时起，妈妈节俭得更是出奇了。

00:00:15.040 --> 00:00:22.630
She began keeping everything that seemed useless in the attic—worn-out clothes, old sheets, broken umbrellas, and some other old items.
她开始把一切看似没用的东西都收进阁楼——穿破的衣服、旧床单、坏伞，还有别的一些旧物件。

00:00:23.430 --> 00:00:26.430
Mom’s favorite saying was “waste not, want not”.
妈妈最爱说的一句话是：“俭以防匮。”

00:00:27.230 --> 00:00:35.530
My brother Josh and I weren’t sure what that meant until what later became known as the “Affair of the Scarecrow”, which left a lasting impression on us.
我和弟弟乔希一直不太明白这句话是什么意思，直到后来被称作“稻草人事件”的那件事发生，它给我们留下了深刻而持久的印象。

00:00:36.330 --> 00:00:41.380
The story began with a pretty hat that Mom received as a gift to protect her from the sun.
事情要从妈妈收到的一顶漂亮帽子说起，那是别人送给她遮阳用的礼物。

00:00:42.180 --> 00:00:47.130
However, Josh and I could see that Mom’s frugal nature and fashion sense were in battle.
可是我和乔希看得出来，妈妈节俭的天性和她的时尚品味正在打架。

00:00:47.930 --> 00:00:54.690
Mom really didn’t want to get rid of the hat—it was new and had a lot of use left in it—but neither could she stand wearing it.
妈妈实在不想把帽子扔掉——帽子是新的，还能用上很久——可她也受不了戴着它。

00:00:55.490 --> 00:00:59.510
She tried to take off the fancy decorations, but they were stuck on tight.
她试着把那些花哨的装饰摘下来，可它们粘得死死的。

00:01:00.310 --> 00:01:02.240
She’d have to find some other solution.
她只好另想办法了。

00:01:03.040 --> 00:01:06.750
Josh and I watched as Mom headed upstairs to the attic with the hat.
我和乔希看着妈妈拿着帽子上楼去了阁楼。

00:01:07.550 --> 00:01:10.250
“Waste not, want not,” she called back down.
“俭以防匮。”她朝楼下喊道。

00:01:11.050 --> 00:01:13.000
We heard boxes being moved around.
我们听见楼上有人在把箱子挪来挪去。

00:01:13.800 --> 00:01:21.330
Moments later, Mom leaned out, holding a flour bag full of straw, and with a mysterious smile, she placed the hat on it.
过了一会儿，妈妈探出身来，手里拿着一个装满稻草的面粉袋，神秘地一笑，把帽子扣在了上面。

00:01:22.130 --> 00:01:24.870
“What is that for?” I asked, confused.
“这是做什么用的？”我一头雾水地问。

00:01:25.670 --> 00:01:27.420
“A scarecrow!” Mom exclaimed.
“稻草人！”妈妈大声说。

00:01:28.220 --> 00:01:29.470
“But not a good one yet.
“不过还不算个好稻草人。

00:01:30.270 --> 00:01:34.240
It needs a strong body to stand firm in the field,” she said with a frown.
它得有个结实的身子，才能在地里站得稳。”她皱着眉头说。

00:01:35.040 --> 00:01:37.230
“And it doesn’t look scary enough,” Josh remarked.
“而且它看上去还不够吓人。”乔希说。

00:01:38.030 --> 00:01:43.130
I had to admit that Josh, though younger than I was, could sometimes be more imaginative.
我不得不承认，乔希虽然比我小，有时却更有想象力。

00:01:43.930 --> 00:01:47.170
Josh and I turned to each other, searching for a good idea.
我和乔希面面相觑，都在想个好主意。

00:01:47.970 --> 00:01:51.230
Soon enough, Josh’s eyes lit up with inspiration.
没过多久，乔希的眼睛一亮，有了灵感。

00:01:52.030 --> 00:01:53.670
“Come with me!” Josh called out.
“跟我来！”乔希喊道。

00:01:54.470 --> 00:01:57.380
In the days that followed, the scarecrow stood tall.
接下来的几天里，稻草人立得笔直。
